# EmailService — Standalone Microservice Build Instructions

**Status:** New, independent build. This is a brand-new solution — there is no existing code to extend.
**How to use this file:** Place this file as `.claude` (or hand it directly as the system/first prompt) inside a **new, empty folder you create yourself**, separate from the AIM/UBIS 2.0 repository. Tell Claude Code to build the full solution described below from scratch in that folder.
**Relationship to AIM:** EmailService is consumed by the AIM module of UBIS 2.0, but this build does **not** need access to AIM's source code. The only thing that matters is the integration contract in §2 — build against that contract and nothing else.

---

## 1. Solution Layout

Same layered convention as AIM (Domain / Application / Infrastructure / WebApi), intentionally thin:

```text
/EmailService
  ├── EmailService.sln
  ├── /Domain
  │     └── /Entities            (EmailDispatchLog)
  ├── /Application
  │     ├── /Interfaces           (IEmailSender)
  │     └── /DTOs                 (SendOtpEmailRequest, SendOtpEmailResult)
  ├── /Infrastructure
  │     ├── /Smtp                 (SmtpEmailSender : IEmailSender)
  │     └── /Messaging             (RabbitMqOtpEmailConsumer)
  └── /WebApi
        ├── /Controllers           (EmailController — internal-only, network-restricted)
        └── Program.cs
```

Target stack: .NET 9 (or match whatever .NET version the rest of UBIS 2.0 is standardized on at build time — confirm before assuming .NET 10), ASP.NET Core minimal hosting, no MVC views needed (this is an internal API only).

---

## 2. Integration Contract (what AIM sends you — build to this exactly)

Two integration options exist upstream — build **both** so AIM can pick either at deploy time, unless told otherwise:

### 2.1 Option A — RabbitMQ consumer (recommended, primary path)

- Queue: `email.otp.send`
- Message payload (JSON) EmailService will receive:
```json
{
  "messageId": "guid",
  "toEmail": "user@ministry.gov.in",
  "toName": "Full Name",
  "otp": "123456",
  "purpose": "LoginMfa | PasswordSetup | PasswordReset",
  "expiryMinutes": 5,
  "requestedAtUtc": "2026-07-06T10:00:00Z"
}
```
- EmailService consumes this queue, sends SMTP mail, writes an `EmailDispatchLog` row (Sent/Failed + provider response). Never call back to AIM synchronously — this is fire-and-forget from the caller's side.

### 2.2 Option B — direct REST (fallback, simpler)

- `POST /internal/email/send-otp` — same JSON body as above.
- Restrict to internal network / mTLS only (do not expose publicly).
- Returns `202 Accepted` immediately; actual send happens async server-side.

---

## 3. `SendOtpEmail` Action (Application layer)

```csharp
// Application/Interfaces/IEmailSender.cs
public interface IEmailSender
{
    Task<Result> SendOtpEmailAsync(SendOtpEmailRequest request, CancellationToken ct);
}
```

Behavior:
- Render a fixed HTML template; subject varies by `purpose`:
  - `LoginMfa` → "Your UBIS 2.0 Login OTP"
  - `PasswordSetup` → "Set Up Your New Password — OTP"
  - `PasswordReset` → "Reset Your Password — OTP"
- Template shows the OTP, the expiry in minutes, and a static "do not share this OTP" warning. No links, no attachments.
- On send, insert `EmailDispatchLog { MessageId, ToEmail (masked in logs, e.g. us***@domain.com), Purpose, Status, ProviderResponseCode, SentAtUtc }`.
- **Critical:** the raw OTP must never be written to `EmailDispatchLog`, application logs, or tracing spans — only whether the send succeeded.

---

## 4. `EmailDispatchLog` Entity

```csharp
// Domain/Entities/EmailDispatchLog.cs
public class EmailDispatchLog
{
    public Guid Id { get; set; }
    public Guid MessageId { get; set; }
    public string ToEmailMasked { get; set; }   // us***@domain.com — never store the full address in logs
    public string Purpose { get; set; }         // LoginMfa | PasswordSetup | PasswordReset
    public string Status { get; set; }          // Sent | Failed
    public string? ProviderResponseCode { get; set; }
    public DateTime SentAtUtc { get; set; }
}
```

Persist this to whichever store is simplest for a thin service — SQL Server table (`emailservice.EmailDispatchLog`) if a database is already provisioned for this service, otherwise a lightweight local store is acceptable for v1; confirm with the team before choosing.

---

## 5. Configuration (`appsettings.json`)

```json
{
  "Smtp": {
    "Host": "",
    "Port": 587,
    "EnableSsl": true,
    "FromAddress": "no-reply@ubis.gov.in",
    "FromName": "UBIS 2.0"
  },
  "RabbitMq": {
    "HostName": "",
    "Queue": "email.otp.send"
  },
  "Internal": {
    "AllowedNetworks": []
  }
}
```
Do not hardcode SMTP credentials or RabbitMQ connection strings — pull from environment/secret store at runtime.

---

## 6. Security & Operational Notes

- `EmailController` (REST fallback path) must be network-restricted — not reachable from outside the internal cluster/VPC.
- Never log the plaintext OTP anywhere in this service, including exception messages and stack traces.
- Mask email addresses (`us***@domain.com`) in all logs and in the `EmailDispatchLog` table.
- This service has no knowledge of users, passwords, or authentication — it only sends what it's told to send. Keep it stateless and swappable.

---

## 7. Implementation Instruction to Claude Code

> Build a new, independent .NET solution named `EmailService` per the folder layout in §1. Implement `IEmailSender`/`SmtpEmailSender`, the `RabbitMqOtpEmailConsumer` for queue `email.otp.send`, and the REST fallback `POST /internal/email/send-otp`, both conforming exactly to the payload in §2. Implement the `EmailDispatchLog` entity and logging behavior in §3–§4, respecting the "never log the raw OTP" rule. Wire configuration per §5. This is a fresh solution — do not assume any existing project structure, and do not reference or depend on the AIM codebase.
